\section{Introduction}

General relativity (GR) describes gravitation geometrically through a Lorentzian metric,
but this mathematical description does not uniquely fix an ontology for the metric.
A useful complementary viewpoint is constitutive: the gravitational state may be
interpreted as a tensorial response state of the vacuum, provided that the ``vacuum''
is not regarded as a material substance embedded in a pre-existing spacetime.  In the
Tensor Polarizable Vacuum (TPV) interpretation, the vacuum \emph{is spacetime}.
The baseline TPV description is therefore simply a constitutive representation of GR
and introduces no new observable physics.

The purpose of the present work is different.  We ask whether the tensor character of
the TPV can be made physically active while preserving an exact GR limit and avoiding
new low-energy gravitational modes.  A natural response channel is the magnetic part of
the Weyl tensor.  Relative to a unit timelike matter four-velocity $u^\mu$,
\begin{equation}
 B_{\mu\nu}
 =
 {}^\star C_{\mu\alpha\nu\beta}u^\alpha u^\beta ,
 \label{eq:Bdef}
\end{equation}
where ${}^\star C_{\mu\alpha\nu\beta}$ denotes the left dual of the Weyl tensor.
Unlike the electric Weyl tensor, $B_{\mu\nu}$ vanishes in static spacetimes relative to
the natural static observers; all static spacetimes are purely electric in this sense
\cite{Hervik2012}.  This property makes $B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}$ attractive for a
matter-gated extension: static stellar structure can remain exactly GR while rotation,
vorticity, and dynamical shear activate a genuinely tensorial response.

Curvature-squared operators generically lead to higher-order field equations if treated
nonperturbatively.  We therefore define active TPV as an EFT, not as a fundamental
fourth-order theory.  Reduction-of-order methods have a long history in gravitational
effective theories \cite{Simon1990,ParkerSimon1993}, and modern work has demonstrated
well-posed low-energy formulations for classes of higher-derivative gravitational EFTs
when corrections remain perturbatively small \cite{DaviesReall2022}.  The physically
retained TPV solution branch is the one continuously connected to GR.

Our central results are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a generally covariant, matter-frame active-TPV action with no independent aether
      field;
\item an exact Noether consistency relation between the TPV metric response and the
      matter force;
\item an exact static-sector theorem: all static GR perfect-fluid solutions and their
      zero-frequency gravitoelectric tidal response are unchanged;
\item an order-reduced field equation whose principal operator is the ordinary
      linearized Einstein--fluid operator;
\item a positive-definite slow-rotation correction to the moment of inertia, implying
      $\Delta I>0$ for $\alpha_B>0$ and $\Gamma\ge 0$;
\item a complete leading $O(\alpha_B\Omega^2)$ quadrupolar response for a benchmark
      neutron star, obtained by sourcing the ordinary Hartle--Thorne $l=2$ system and
      matching to the unchanged GR exterior.
\end{enumerate}

The quadrupole calculation is performed from the full variational source rather than
from the $P_2$ coefficient of $B^2$ alone.  In particular, the $r\theta$ constraint,
the trace-free angular constraint, and the TPV modification of the fluid first integral
must all be retained.

Throughout we use $G=c=1$ and metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$.

\section{Covariant active-TPV theory}

\subsection{Matter frame}

A general relativistic perfect fluid can be formulated covariantly using comoving
matter coordinates or equivalent variational variables \cite{Brown1993}.  We denote the
matter fields collectively by $\psi^A$ and assume that they define a normalized physical
four-velocity
\begin{equation}
 u^\mu u_\mu=-1.
\end{equation}
The important point is that $u^\mu$ is an existing matter variable.  No additional
aether, preferred foliation, or independent timelike vector field is introduced.

For a barotropic perfect fluid,
\begin{equation}
 T_{\mu\nu}=(\rho+p)u_\mu u_\nu+p g_{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
The construction can accommodate vortical flow; hypersurface orthogonality of
$u^\mu$ is not assumed in the covariant theory.

\subsection{Action}

We define
\begin{equation}
 S_{\rm TPV}
 =
 S_{\rm EH}
 +
 S_m[g,\psi]
 +
 S_B ,
 \label{eq:action}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
 S_{\rm EH}
 =
 \frac{\mpl^2}{2}\int \dd^4x\sqrt{-g}\,R,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 S_B
 =
 \frac{\mpl^2}{2}\lambda
 \int \dd^4x\sqrt{-g}\,
 \Gamma(\rho,p,\ldots)\,
 B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu},
 \qquad
 \lambda\equiv\frac{\alpha_B}{\Lambda_Q^2}.
 \label{eq:SB}
\end{equation}
Here $\alpha_B$ is dimensionless, $\Lambda_Q$ is the EFT scale, and $\Gamma$ is a
dimensionless covariant gate built from local matter scalars.  We require
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma\rightarrow0
 \quad\text{smoothly as matter}\rightarrow0,
 \label{eq:gatevac}
\end{equation}
so the exterior vacuum field equations reduce exactly to those of GR.

For numerical illustrations below we use the fiducial gate
\begin{align}
 \Gamma(\mathcal D,\zeta)
 &=
 \exp\Bigg[
 -\ln2\Bigg(
 \left(\frac{\mathcal D_-}{\mathcal D}\right)^{0.4}
 +
 \left(\frac{\mathcal D}{\mathcal D_+}\right)^{0.4}
 +
 \left(\frac{\zeta_\star}{\zeta}\right)^4
 \Bigg)
 \Bigg],
 \label{eq:gate}
\\
 \mathcal D
 &=
 (8\pi\rho)^4\left(\frac{p}{\rho}\right)^3,
 \\
 \zeta
 &=
 \frac{(1-3p/\rho)^2}{1+3(p/\rho)^2},
\end{align}
with
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal D_-=10^{-11}\ {\rm km^{-8}},\qquad
 \mathcal D_+=5\times10^{-9}\ {\rm km^{-8}},\qquad
 \zeta_\star=0.08.
 \label{eq:gateparams}
\end{equation}
This particular gate is a benchmark choice rather than a unique prediction of the
framework.

\section{Noether consistency}

Let
\begin{equation}
 I_B[g,\psi]
 =
 \int \dd^4x\sqrt{-g}\,\Gamma B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
Its variation may be written
\begin{equation}
 \delta I_B
 =
 \int \dd^4x\sqrt{-g}
 \left[
 \frac12\calK^{\mu\nu}\delta g_{\mu\nu}
 +
 \calF_A\delta\psi^A
 \right].
 \label{eq:variation}
\end{equation}
For a matter representation based on scalar comoving coordinates, an infinitesimal
diffeomorphism generated by $\xi^\mu$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \delta_\xi g_{\mu\nu}=2\nabla_{(\mu}\xi_{\nu)},\qquad
 \delta_\xi\psi^A=\xi^\mu\nabla_\mu\psi^A.
\end{equation}
Diffeomorphism invariance of $I_B$, followed by integration by parts, gives the
off-shell identity
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \nabla_\mu\calK^\mu{}_\nu
 =
 \calF_A\nabla_\nu\psi^A .
 }
 \label{eq:noether}
\end{equation}
Thus the TPV correction to the gravitational equation and the TPV force on matter are
not independent modifications.  They are linked by the same generally covariant
interaction.

With conventions fixed by Eq.~\eqref{eq:variation}, the field equations may be written
schematically as
\begin{equation}
 G_{\mu\nu}
 =
 \mpl^{-2}T_{\mu\nu}
 +
 \frac{\lambda}{2}\calK_{\mu\nu},
 \label{eq:metricfield}
\end{equation}
together with the corrected matter equations
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal E_A^{(0)}
 +
 \frac{\mpl^2\lambda}{2}\calF_A
 =0.
 \label{eq:matterfield}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:noether} ensures compatibility of the matter equations with the
contracted Bianchi identity.

\section{Exact static GR sector}

The central structural property of Eq.~\eqref{eq:SB} is the vanishing of the magnetic
Weyl tensor in a static spacetime relative to the static observers.  In particular,
\begin{equation}
 B_{\mu\nu}=0
 \label{eq:Bzero}
\end{equation}
for a static perfect-fluid configuration whose four-velocity is aligned with the static
Killing congruence \cite{Hervik2012}.

On such a background,
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}=0.
\end{equation}
Its first variation is also zero:
\begin{align}
 \delta\!\left(\Gamma B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}\right)
 &=
 (\delta\Gamma)B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}
 +
 2\Gamma B^{\mu\nu}\delta B_{\mu\nu}
 \nonumber\\
 &=0.
 \label{eq:firstvarzero}
\end{align}
Therefore
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \calK_{\mu\nu}=0,\qquad \calF_A=0
 }
 \label{eq:staticzero}
\end{equation}
on every such configuration.

It follows immediately that
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \text{every static GR perfect-fluid solution is an exact active-TPV solution}.
 }
 \label{eq:staticthm}
\end{equation}
The Tolman--Oppenheimer--Volkoff mass--radius sequence is therefore unchanged for
fixed equation of state.

The same observation applies to a strictly static gravitoelectric tidal deformation.
The deformed spacetime remains static and hence purely electric relative to its static
matter congruence.  The $B^2$ operator vanishes identically, so the zero-frequency
electric tidal Love number is unchanged:
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \Lambda_E^{\rm TPV}=\Lambda_E^{\rm GR}.
 }
 \label{eq:lovezero}
\end{equation}
This should not be confused with a statement about dynamical tides or gravitomagnetic
Love numbers, where $B_{\mu\nu}$ need not vanish.  Standard relativistic electric Love
numbers are reviewed, for example, in Refs.~\cite{Hinderer2008,BinningtonPoisson2009}.

\section{Order-reduced effective dynamics}

If Eq.~\eqref{eq:metricfield} is interpreted as an exact higher-derivative theory, a
generic vortical matter frame can mix electric and magnetic Weyl components and expose
higher time derivatives.  We therefore define active TPV perturbatively as an EFT.

Collect the metric and matter fields into $\Phi^I$ and write the equations as
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E_0[\Phi]
 +
 \lambda\,\mathbb E_B[\Phi]
 =0,
 \label{eq:fulleq}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb E_0$ denotes the Einstein--fluid equations.  The low-energy branch is
expanded as
\begin{equation}
 \Phi=\Phi_0+\lambda\Phi_1+O(\lambda^2).
 \label{eq:phiexp}
\end{equation}
At successive orders,
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E_0[\Phi_0]&=0,
 \label{eq:order0}\\
 D\mathbb E_0[\Phi_0]\cdot\Phi_1
 &=
 -\mathbb E_B[\Phi_0].
 \label{eq:order1}
\end{align}
Equation~\eqref{eq:order1} is the defining first-order TPV equation.

The principal differential operator acting on the unknown correction $\Phi_1$ is exactly
the ordinary linearized Einstein--fluid operator.  Higher derivatives generated by the
$B^2$ operator occur only in the known source evaluated on $\Phi_0$.  The perturbative
branch therefore requires no additional low-energy initial data beyond those of GR and
the original fluid.

This is the standard logic of perturbative constraints/reduction of order in
higher-derivative EFTs \cite{Simon1990,ParkerSimon1993}.  The additional roots of the
unreduced finite higher-derivative truncation are nonanalytic in the EFT coefficient and
are not retained as physical low-energy solutions.  We do not claim that the unreduced
fourth-order equations define a UV-complete fundamental theory.  Rather, the regime of
interest is characterized schematically by
\begin{equation}
 |\lambda|\,\Gamma\,k^2\ll1,
 \qquad
 |\lambda|\,\Gamma\,|\mathcal R|\ll1,
 \label{eq:EFTdomain}
\end{equation}
where $k$ and $\mathcal R$ are characteristic wave-number and curvature scales.
Related work has shown that higher-derivative gravitational EFTs can admit well-posed
formulations when the EFT corrections remain perturbatively small
\cite{DaviesReall2022}.

\section{Slow rotation}

\subsection{GR background}

For uniform slow rotation, adopt Hartle's metric at first order in angular velocity
$\Omega$,
\begin{equation}
 \dd s^2
 =
 -N(r)^2\dd t^2
 +
 A(r)^2\dd r^2
 +
 r^2\left[
 \dd\theta^2+
 \sin^2\theta\left(\dd\phi-\omega(r)\dd t\right)^2
 \right]
 +O(\Omega^2).
 \label{eq:hartlemetric}
\end{equation}
Define
\begin{equation}
 \bar\omega(r)\equiv\Omega-\omega(r),
 \qquad
 j(r)\equiv\frac{1}{N(r)A(r)}.
\end{equation}
On the exact GR spherical background, Hartle's first-order equation is
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\dd}{\dd r}
 \left(
 r^4j\frac{\dd\bar\omega}{\dd r}
 \right)
 +
 4r^3j'\bar\omega
 =0.
 \label{eq:hartle}
\end{equation}
The exterior solution is
\begin{equation}
 \bar\omega_{\rm ext}
 =
 \Omega-\frac{2J}{r^3},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 I_{\rm GR}\equiv\frac{J}{\Omega}.
\end{equation}
These are the standard Hartle slow-rotation relations
\cite{Hartle1967,HartleThorne1968}.

\subsection{Magnetic-Weyl functional}

To $O(\Omega)$ the magnetic Weyl tensor is nonzero.  After angular reduction, the
quadratic TPV contribution can be written in terms of the GR frame-dragging solution as
\begin{equation}
 S_B^{(2)}
 =
 \pi\mpl^2\lambda
 \int \dd t\,\dd r\,
 \frac{\Gamma A r^2}{N}
 \left[
 \frac{\bar\omega'^2}{A^2}
 +
 \frac{8}{3}F[\bar\omega]^2
 \right],
 \label{eq:SB1d}
\end{equation}
where, after using the GR Hartle equation and TOV identities,
\begin{equation}
 F[\bar\omega]
 =
 -\frac{\bar\omega'}{2A^2}
 +
 \left(
 \frac{3m}{r^2}
 +
 4\pi r p
 \right)\bar\omega .
 \label{eq:Fdef}
\end{equation}
The response is therefore localized to matter through $\Gamma$ and vanishes exactly in
the exterior.

Write
\begin{equation}
 \bar\omega
 =
 \bar\omega_0+\lambda\bar\omega_1+O(\lambda^2),
\end{equation}
where $\bar\omega_0$ satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:hartle}.  The correction
$\bar\omega_1$ obeys an inhomogeneous equation with the same Hartle operator:
\begin{equation}
 \calH[\bar\omega_1]
 =
 S_B[\bar\omega_0],
 \qquad
 \calH[y]\equiv(r^4jy')'+4r^3j'y.
 \label{eq:inhomHartle}
\end{equation}
Thus no additional radial homogeneous mode or boundary condition is introduced at
$O(\lambda)$.

\subsection{Positive-definite moment-of-inertia correction}

Self-adjointness of the Hartle operator and matching to the unchanged vacuum exterior
give the leading correction
\begin{align}
 \Delta I
 &=
 \frac{\alpha_B}{4\Lambda_Q^2\Omega^2}
 \int_0^R\dd r\,
 \frac{\Gamma A r^2}{N}
 \Bigg[
 \frac{\bar\omega_0'^2}{A^2}
 \nonumber\\
 &\hspace{2.7cm}
 +
 \frac{8}{3}
 \left(
 -\frac{\bar\omega_0'}{2A^2}
 +
 \left[
 \frac{3m}{r^2}+4\pi rp
 \right]\bar\omega_0
 \right)^2
 \Bigg].
 \label{eq:deltaI}
\end{align}
Every factor in the integrand is nonnegative for a regular stellar background if
$\Gamma\ge0$.  Hence
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \alpha_B>0,\ \Gamma\ge0
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \Delta I>0
 }
 \label{eq:sign}
\end{equation}
unless the magnetic-Weyl response vanishes identically.

This sign is an analytic prediction of the operator and does not depend on the detailed
equation of state.

\section{Numerical benchmark}

For the quadrupole calculation we use a smooth generalized piecewise-polytropic
representation of SLy4 with continuous sound speed, following the GPP construction of
O'Boyle \emph{et al.}~\cite{OBoyle2020}.  The static background is an ordinary GR
solution by Eq.~\eqref{eq:staticthm}.  For the $1.4\,M_\odot$ benchmark we obtain
\begin{equation}
 M=1.4000000\,M_\odot,\qquad
 R=11.6269\ {\rm km},
 \qquad
 I_{\rm GR}=100.2907\ {\rm km^3}.
 \label{eq:benchmarkGR}
\end{equation}
The corresponding dimensionless moment of inertia is
\begin{equation}
 \bar I_{\rm GR}\equiv\frac{I_{\rm GR}}{M^3}=11.3508.
\end{equation}

We take
\begin{equation}
 \Lambda_Q^2
 =
 5.9333\times10^{-2}\ {\rm km^{-2}},
 \qquad
 \alpha_B=0.05,
 \label{eq:benchmarkcoupling}
\end{equation}
together with Eqs.~\eqref{eq:gate}--\eqref{eq:gateparams}.  Integrating the GR TOV
and Hartle equations and evaluating Eq.~\eqref{eq:deltaI} gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \frac{\Delta I}{I_{\rm GR}}
 =
 7.936\times10^{-4}
 }
 \label{eq:numericalshift}
\end{equation}
at the fiducial point.

The calculation was implemented in two forms: direct evaluation of the
positive-definite integral Eq.~\eqref{eq:deltaI}, and direct integration of the
inhomogeneous Hartle equation Eq.~\eqref{eq:inhomHartle} followed by exterior
matching.  The agreement of the two implementations is used as an internal check of
the first-order rotational sector.

The stellar-scale derivative-expansion parameter is
\begin{equation}
 \epsilon_R
 \sim
 \frac{\alpha_B\Gamma_{\max}}
 {\Lambda_Q^2R^2},
 \label{eq:epsR}
\end{equation}
which is of order a few $\times10^{-3}$ for the benchmark.  The rotational
calculation is therefore well inside the intended perturbative regime.

\section{Observable structure}

The active TPV operator produces a distinctive separation between static and
gravitomagnetic stellar observables:
\begin{align}
 M(R)_{\rm TPV}
 &=M(R)_{\rm GR},
 \\
 \Lambda_{E,\rm TPV}
 &=\Lambda_{E,\rm GR},
 \\
 I_{\rm TPV}
 &>I_{\rm GR}
 \qquad(\alpha_B>0).
 \label{eq:signature}
\end{align}
The first two equalities are exact for the static sectors described above; the third is
the leading slow-rotation result.

This pattern cannot be reproduced merely by replacing one static barotropic EOS with
another while keeping the same static background and the same zero-frequency electric
tidal response.  The new effect is tied specifically to the magnetic/tensor response of
spacetime relative to moving matter.

\section{Spin-induced quadrupole}

\subsection{Hartle--Thorne response problem}

At $O(\Omega^2)$ the standard Hartle--Thorne problem contains even-parity
$l=0$ and $l=2$ deformations~\cite{Hartle1967,HartleThorne1968}.  The first-order
magnetic Weyl tensor is $O(\Omega)$, so $B^2$ is $O(\Omega^2)$ and contains only
$l=0$ and $l=2$ angular structure.  The quadrupole source, however, is the complete
variation of
\begin{equation}
 \sqrt{-g}\,\Gamma B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
not simply the $P_2$ coefficient of $B^2$.

We therefore expand the action with respect to the independent $l=2$ metric
perturbations and retain the corresponding off-diagonal $r\theta$ and trace-free
angular constraints.  The matter-frame normalization and the gate variation are
included simultaneously.  In the fluid sector the TPV term modifies the rigid-rotation
first integral.  These contributions provide a compact interior source for the
ordinary GR Hartle--Thorne $l=2$ operator.

Because $\Gamma=0$ in vacuum, the exterior remains exactly the GR Hartle--Thorne
solution.  In Hartle's convention it may be parameterized by $J$ and a second exterior
constant $K$, with the quadrupole magnitude
\begin{equation}
 Q=\frac{J^2}{M}+\frac{8}{5}KM^3.
 \label{eq:QHartle}
\end{equation}
Writing
\begin{equation}
 J=J_0+\lambda J_1,\qquad
 K=K_0+\lambda K_1,
\end{equation}
gives
\begin{equation}
 Q_1=
 \frac{2J_0J_1}{M}
 +\frac{8}{5}M^3K_1.
 \label{eq:Q1}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Baseline validation}

Before adding the TPV source, the reconstructed GR $l=2$ solver gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\bar Q_{\rm GR}=4.96928},
 \qquad
 \bar Q\equiv\frac{QM}{J^2}.
 \label{eq:qbargr}
\end{equation}
For the same star $\bar I_{\rm GR}=11.3508$.  This is consistent with the standard
slow-rotation I--Q relation of Yagi and Yunes~\cite{YagiYunes2013} and provides an
independent validation of the GR Hartle--Thorne implementation.

\subsection{TPV quadrupole shift}

The full sourced calculation gives, per unit $\lambda$ in the normalization used for
the slow-rotation integration,
\begin{equation}
 K_1=1.516,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 Q_1=30.59.
\end{equation}
At the fiducial coupling Eq.~\eqref{eq:benchmarkcoupling},
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \frac{\Delta Q}{Q}
 =
 (1.066\pm0.005)\times10^{-3}.
 }
 \label{eq:deltaQ}
\end{equation}
The sign convention in Eq.~\eqref{eq:deltaQ} is Hartle's positive quadrupole-magnitude
convention.  The quoted uncertainty is a numerical source-generation and convergence
estimate; it is not an EFT truncation, EOS, or gate-model uncertainty.

Three independent finite-difference source resolutions give
\begin{equation}
 1.0629,\quad1.0673,\quad1.0662
 \qquad\times10^{-3},
\end{equation}
and variation of the inner integration radius gives a substantially smaller change.
The source also satisfies the regular-center STF relations among the radial,
$r\theta$, and trace-free angular components.

The physical quadrupole magnitude therefore increases at fixed $\Omega$.  However,
the normalized quadrupole behaves differently because
$J=I\Omega$ also increases.  To first order,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\Delta\bar Q}{\bar Q}
 =
 \frac{\Delta Q}{Q}
 -
 2\frac{\Delta I}{I},
\end{equation}
and hence
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \frac{\Delta\bar Q}{\bar Q}
 =
 -5.21\times10^{-4},
 \qquad
 \bar Q_{\rm TPV}\simeq4.96669.
 }
 \label{eq:deltaqbar}
\end{equation}

The physical $Q$ shift may be decomposed linearly into three contributions:
\begin{align}
 \left.\frac{\Delta Q}{Q}\right|_{\delta\omega}
 &\simeq +4.26\times10^{-4},\\
 \left.\frac{\Delta Q}{Q}\right|_{\rm direct\ metric}
 &\simeq +7.45\times10^{-4},\\
 \left.\frac{\Delta Q}{Q}\right|_{\rm fluid/gate}
 &\simeq -1.05\times10^{-4}.
\end{align}
Their sum reproduces Eq.~\eqref{eq:deltaQ} within the numerical integration error.

\subsection{I--Love--Q displacement}

Active TPV therefore modifies both rotational members of the I--Love--Q trio while
leaving the zero-frequency electric Love number unchanged.  At the benchmark,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\Delta\bar I}{\bar I}=+7.94\times10^{-4},
 \qquad
 \frac{\Delta\bar Q}{\bar Q}=-5.21\times10^{-4}.
\end{equation}
Linearizing the central GR I--Q fit of Ref.~\cite{YagiYunes2013} at the benchmark
would instead associate the measured $\Delta\bar I$ with a normalized quadrupole
change of approximately $+9.91\times10^{-4}$.  The TPV point is therefore displaced
from that central GR fit by approximately
\begin{equation}
 \Delta\ln\bar Q\big|_{\rm TPV-GR\ fit}
 \simeq-1.51\times10^{-3}.
 \label{eq:IQdisp}
\end{equation}
This comparison is a theoretical diagnostic; determining whether such a displacement is
observationally resolvable requires a separate analysis of EOS scatter and measurement
uncertainties.

\section{Discussion}

The construction presented here is intentionally modest in its claims.  It is not a
microscopic model of vacuum constituents and does not provide a UV completion of
gravity.  Rather, it is a covariant low-energy constitutive extension of GR designed to
make the tensor response of a TPV physically active in matter while retaining an exact
vacuum and static GR sector.

Several properties distinguish the proposal from a generic curvature-squared
modification:
\begin{itemize}
\item the operator is gated by matter and vanishes smoothly in vacuum;
\item the timelike direction entering $B_{\mu\nu}$ is supplied by physical matter, not
      by an independent preferred-frame field;
\item the theory is defined perturbatively by reduction of order, so the physical branch
      inherits the Einstein--fluid initial-data count;
\item all static perfect-fluid backgrounds remain exact GR solutions;
\item the leading rotational correction has a definite sign.
\end{itemize}

The benchmark size in Eq.~\eqref{eq:numericalshift} is small.  That is not a defect:
the primary theoretical result is the existence of a controlled constitutive response
that cannot be absorbed into a static EOS remapping.  Observational constraints and
parameter inference require a broader survey over masses, EOSs, gate choices, and
$\Lambda_Q$.

The most important next calculations are:
(i) an independent symbolic or automatic-differentiation reconstruction of the
$l=2$ TPV source and a projected Noether/gauge-consistency check;
(ii) dynamical and gravitomagnetic tidal responses;
(iii) oscillation and gravitational-wave spectra using the same covariant EFT;
(iv) a systematic hyperbolicity analysis of a chosen order-reduced gauge-fixed
formulation; and
(v) observational bounds on $\alpha_B/\Lambda_Q^2$.

\section{Conclusion}

We have constructed a physically active Tensor Polarizable Vacuum EFT in which the
vacuum is identified with spacetime and the leading new constitutive response is
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
The matter four-velocity determines the local magnetic-Weyl frame, while the gate
confines the modification to material regions.

Diffeomorphism covariance gives an exact Noether relation between the modified metric
and matter equations.  Defining the theory perturbatively by reduction of order ensures
that the $O(\alpha_B)$ corrections are governed by the ordinary linearized
Einstein--fluid operator and therefore do not require additional low-energy initial
data.

The theory possesses an exact static GR sector.  In particular,
\begin{equation}
 B_{\mu\nu}=0
\end{equation}
for static stellar configurations, so the mass--radius relation and zero-frequency
electric tidal Love number remain unchanged.  Rotation activates the response.  In
Hartle's slow-rotation approximation the leading change in the moment of inertia is a
positive-definite functional of the GR stellar solution.  Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_B>0
 \quad\Rightarrow\quad
 I_{\rm TPV}>I_{\rm GR}.
\end{equation}
For the illustrative $1.4\,M_\odot$ smooth-SLy4 benchmark and fiducial coupling
used here, the moment-of-inertia shift is
$7.94\times10^{-4}$.  The completed $l=2$ calculation gives
$\Delta Q/Q=(1.066\pm0.005)\times10^{-3}$ and
$\Delta\bar Q/\bar Q=-5.21\times10^{-4}$.

The resulting phenomenological pattern,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \text{static GR response unchanged}
 \quad+\quad
 \text{modified rotational tensor response},
 }
\end{equation}
is the principal result.  It provides a concrete example in which the ``tensor'' in
Tensor Polarizable Vacuum becomes physically active without modifying the static
stellar background or introducing a new retained low-energy gravitational branch.
The benchmark is displaced from the central GR I--Q relation while retaining the GR
zero-frequency electric Love number.  An independent symbolic reconstruction of the
second-order TPV source remains desirable before precision phenomenology, but the
leading slow-rotation quadrupole response is numerically closed.




